              "text": "Because the phrase *\"how much percent do mutual fund managers make\"* can mean two different things, the answer is broken down into both perspectives:\n\n1. **The percentage fee they charge you** to manage the mutual fund (the Expense Ratio).\n2. **The percentage/amount the actual individual person (the Portfolio Manager) makes** in personal compensation. \n\n---\n\n### Part 1: What percentage do mutual fund companies charge? (Fees)\nWhen you invest in a mutual fund, the fund management company charges a recurring annual fee expressed as a percentage of your total investment. This is called the **Expense Ratio**.\n\n* **Actively Managed Mutual Funds:** If a human manager is actively researching, buying, and selling stocks to try to beat the market, the fee is higher. \n  * **Management Fee:** Typically **0.50% to 1.00%** of assets under management (AUM). This part directly compensates the management and research team.\n  * **Total Expense Ratio (TER):** Typically **0.60% to 1.50%**. This includes the management fee plus administrative, compliance, recordkeeping, and marketing costs (12b-1 fees).\n* **Passively Managed Index Funds:** If the fund simply tracks an index (like the S&P 500), the fees are minimal because there is no active manager. \n  * **Total Expense Ratio (TER):** Typically **0.02% to 0.20%**. \n\n#### Do they make a percentage of the profits?\n**No, usually not.** Unlike hedge fund managers\u2014who famously use a \"2 and 20\" model (taking a 2% flat fee plus **20% of all profits** earned)\u2014traditional mutual funds are heavily regulated and almost **never charge performance fees**. They make their percentage fee regardless of whether the fund wins or loses money in a given year.\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: How much do individual mutual fund managers personally make? (Compensation)\nIf you are wondering how much money the actual human being running the fund takes home, they do **not** directly pocket the 1% management fee. They are salaried employees of the asset management firm (e.g., Fidelity, Vanguard, BlackRock).\n\nOver **95% of mutual fund managers** are compensated through a **Salary-plus-Bonus** structure:\n\n* **The Base Salary:** Usually a fixed six-figure salary depending on their experience, ranging from **$150,000 to $350,000+**. \n* **The Performance Bonus (The bulk of their pay):** About **75% to 80%** of mutual fund managers have their bonuses tied directly to fund performance. \n  * Their bonus is typically calculated based on **how much they beat their benchmark** (like the S&P 500) over a rolling 1-year, 3-year, or 5-year period. \n  * They may also receive **profit-sharing** based on the overall profitability of the investment firm, which is tied to the total assets the firm manages. \n\n#### What is their total annual take-home pay?\nBecause of these performance bonuses, a manager\u2019s compensation varies heavily based on the size of the fund they manage and their track record:\n* **Median Compensation:** The median mutual fund manager in the U.S. earns about **$462,000 per year** in total compensation.\n* **Average (Mean) Compensation:** The average is much higher, around **$1.1 million per year**. This is because elite managers of massive, multi-billion-dollar funds can earn multi-million dollar annual bonuses.",
